WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:03.220
- [Kim] Hi this is Kim from Khan Academy.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:05.327
Today I'm learning about
the Sixth Amendment

00:00:05.327 --> 00:00:07.310
to the U.S. Constitution.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:09.940
One of four Amendments
in the Bill of Rights

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.190
that concerns the rights of the accused,

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:15.740
the Sixth Amendment guarantees
defendants in criminal cases

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.100
the right to a speedy and public trial.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.700
To learn more about the Sixth Amendment,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.137
I sought out the help of two experts.

00:00:23.137 --> 00:00:26.700
Stephanos Bibas is a
United States Circuit Judge

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.300
for the U.S. Court of Appeals
for the Third Circuit.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.710
He's also a leading scholar
of criminal procedure.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.272
Jeffrey Fisher is Professor of Law

00:00:35.272 --> 00:00:39.260
and Co-director of the Supreme
Court Litigation Clinic

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.820
at Stanford Law School.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.289
So, Judge Bibas, in the
case of the Bill of Rights,

00:00:44.289 --> 00:00:46.148
the Framers were trying

00:00:46.148 --> 00:00:50.780
to prevent particular
abuses of the government.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.440
So what was the historical
background that led them

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.880
to want to protect these
rights in particular?

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.570
- [Bibas] In England in the late 17th

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:00.780
and early 18th century,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.210
there was a series of treason
trials other political trials

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:06.360
and the king had done some things

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.461
that bypassed the usual mode
of trial in criminal cases.

00:01:10.461 --> 00:01:13.490
The Anglo-American system
is known as the Common Law.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.556
It involves live testimony in open court

00:01:16.556 --> 00:01:19.300
before a jury of your peers.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.270
And the jury ultimately
decides did you do it,

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:25.410
were you blame-worthy, do
you deserve the punishment.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.790
The Crown had railroaded some
of its political opponents,

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.500
people like Sir Walter Raleigh,

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.625
people accused in the Popish Plots

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:36.270
during the strife between
Catholics and Protestants.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.800
And some of these convictions
were later understood

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.030
to have been wrongful ones,

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:45.110
and so the colonists in
England and then in America

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:48.036
insisted on protecting
against various ways

00:01:48.036 --> 00:01:51.950
of diminishing or abridging
the trial that would be speedy,

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.020
that would be public, that
would be before a jury,

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.940
that would have an evenly
matched prosecution and defense,

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.890
and would have an opportunity
to look the witness in the eye

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.840
and confront him the way it
was always done in England,

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.880
as opposed to on continental Europe.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.860
- [Fisher] There's a varied
history, primarily in England,

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.860
of certain periods of time
where people were prosecuted

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:18.560
in ways that the Founders
deemed fundamentally unfair.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.961
Probably the most notorious example,

00:02:19.961 --> 00:02:22.010
was the Star Chamber.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:26.030
That was a moniker given
to a king-run tribunal

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.650
decades before the founding,

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.250
where people were picked up and arrested

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.830
and then sentenced to criminal punishment

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:36.140
and sometimes to death without
ever having an opportunity

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:37.730
to challenge the charges against them

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:38.860
and sometimes not even knowing

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:40.730
what the charges were against them.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:44.583
And so that kind of a travesty
of justice and abuse of power

00:02:44.583 --> 00:02:48.420
was probably the foremost
thing in the Framers' minds.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.020
- [Bibas] This was partly for the benefit

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.760
of the defendant or the accused.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:53.380
It also was very importantly

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.710
about protecting the citizen's right

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.500
to control criminal justice,

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.650
that it wasn't the king who would do this,

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.930
and the King couldn't just
let his royal officers off.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.580
So it's very important

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:08.040
to look at the Declaration
of Independence.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.400
A bunch of the complaints
in the Declaration

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.920
are complaints that the King
is bypassing these usual modes,

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.810
that he's protecting his
soldiers by a mock trial

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.440
from punishment for murders
which they should commit

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.290
on the inhabitants of the states,

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:24.970
that he's depriving us in many cases

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.100
of the benefits of trial by jury,

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.055
and that he has made the judges subject

00:03:30.055 --> 00:03:33.120
to his manipulation and pleasure

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.120
because they don't have stable salaries.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.785
So, the colonists were very suspicious

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:42.830
of the King's manipulation
of criminal justice.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.410
- [Kim] So how does the
Sixth Amendment inform

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.890
how the U.S. legal system works today?

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:50.390
- [Fisher] Well, the first
important thing to understand is

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.690
that when the Sixth Amendment was drafted,

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.446
just like all the other
first ten Amendments,

00:03:55.446 --> 00:03:58.580
they applied primarily only
to the Federal Government,

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.700
not to state governments.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.820
But in the 60s and 70s,

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.210
the Supreme Court applied

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:08.000
all of these rights one
by one to the states.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.890
So now, because the rights apply

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:13.150
to both the Federal Government
and to state governments,

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.110
the Sixth Amendment applies

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:19.050
every single time somebody
is charged with a crime.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.333
And so that starts

00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:25.333
with knowing what you're
being charged with,

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:28.670
whether it's by what's known
as a Grand Jury Indictment

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:31.400
or some other method of accusing somebody

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.190
of committing a crime.

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:37.670
And it continues on with
the right to an attorney

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.480
to represent the accused,

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.870
and goes all the way through
the decision-making process

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:46.650
with how the jury decides
whether to convict or not.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:47.850
- [Kim] This is fascinating.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.660
There are a couple of things that I see

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:50.770
in the Sixth Amendment

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:54.360
that seem like they might be
a little difficult to define,

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.950
particularly the idea of a speedy trial.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:01.092
So what actually counts as speedy?

00:05:01.092 --> 00:05:05.929
- [Bibas] The speedy trial
component is not well-defined.

00:05:05.929 --> 00:05:09.810
The Supreme Court has ultimately said,

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.500
well, we're gonna balance
a bunch of factors.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:14.142
How long is the delay?

00:05:14.142 --> 00:05:16.510
What's the reason for the delay?

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:20.190
Has the defendant shown any
prejudice from the delay?

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.190
How long did it take

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:24.730
after the defendant
requested the speedy trial?

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.220
And very rarely does the court

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:29.180
actually throw out a case based on that.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:30.900
What happens more often is

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:32.710
that the defendant makes a request

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:34.490
and then judges really try

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:37.580
to push the case along to be faster.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.030
So the court has turned
it into really focusing

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.750
on the defendant's
interest in something fast,

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.980
and that matters a lot when
a defendant doesn't make bail

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:48.172
and is being held in jail pending trial.

00:05:48.172 --> 00:05:52.603
So those cases will go to
trial in a matter of months.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.230
A case where someone is out on bail

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.713
can easily take a year or two

00:05:57.713 --> 00:06:00.830
before it ultimately proceeds to trial.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:03.833
- [Fisher] What's speedy or
what's reasonable in 1791,

00:06:03.833 --> 00:06:08.833
might not be speedy or reasonable in 2017.

00:06:10.916 --> 00:06:14.779
So what the courts have said
with respect to speedy is

00:06:14.779 --> 00:06:17.020
there's not a bright line rule,

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:21.580
say one year, five years,
whatever it might be

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:22.770
that somebody has to be brought

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.930
to trial after being charged.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:27.631
Rather what the court does is it considers

00:06:27.631 --> 00:06:32.599
how diligently the prosecution
has tried to pursue the case

00:06:32.599 --> 00:06:34.300
in light of other factors,

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.290
such as the complexity of the case,

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:39.160
whether the defendant
himself wants the case

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:40.160
to go to trial soon,

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.100
or whether the defendant
himself has agreed

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:46.350
to certain delays or said
that he doesn't mind them,

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.870
and whether there's
prejudice that has occurred,

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.010
which is a legal way of saying

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:54.010
whether somebody's been harmed
or injured by the delay.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.270
Say, for example, a key
witness has now died

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:57.840
or fled the jurisdiction.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.740
So it's flexible words like
speedy in essence translates

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.680
some measure of discretion
and flexibility to the courts.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:11.164
- [Kim] Why is our legal process
so much slower than it was

00:07:11.164 --> 00:07:13.520
in the 18th century?

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.530
- [Bibas] Okay, so that connects up

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:19.140
to the second set of
rights we've constructed

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.570
around what makes
something a public trial.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:24.589
One of the main things
there is the jury right.

00:07:24.589 --> 00:07:27.566
Juries in the 18th century,

00:07:27.566 --> 00:07:29.930
we didn't have a jury selection process.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:33.160
The first 12 people who
didn't know the plaintiff

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:35.640
or the defendant, or the
victim, or whoever it was,

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.820
would be impaneled and
they would serve all day

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:40.690
and they'd hear all the
cases that came that day.

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:44.870
And you didn't have lawyers
involved in most of these cases.

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:46.510
Usually it was the victim

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:48.530
against the defendant himself or herself,

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:51.403
no lawyers speaking for either side.

00:07:52.787 --> 00:07:54.140
That was the norm.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:55.610
The defendant had a
right to hire a lawyer,

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:58.500
but usually couldn't afford it and didn't.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.330
There weren't rules of evidence.

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:04.070
There weren't complicated motions.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.410
There weren't technical jury instructions.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:08.860
So the trials themselves took, as I said,

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:10.400
an hour, two hours.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:12.940
Pre-trial proceedings did not involve lots

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:15.050
of legal motions because there
were no lawyers to run them.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.460
The judges themselves often
didn't have legal training.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.300
There were no law schools in America.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:21.020
So when you have a system like that,

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:22.170
it's not that cumbersome.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.700
There's very little
incentive to bypass it.

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:26.910
And you don't have professional
lawyers who are looking

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:30.519
to get this case done with
and get on to the next case,

00:08:30.519 --> 00:08:33.420
or who have seen enough cases

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:35.440
that they can bargain back and forth

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:37.320
and strike a bargain for this case

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.090
that's about where the
previous cases came out.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:42.250
So you didn't have an
incentive for plea bargaining.

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:43.901
It wasn't slow enough.

00:08:43.901 --> 00:08:44.734
You didn't have the experts

00:08:44.734 --> 00:08:47.020
who'd take to run plea bargaining.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:51.146
And you didn't have the lack
of investment in your own case

00:08:51.146 --> 00:08:53.100
that makes plea bargaining possible,

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.939
or the technical rules.

00:08:54.939 --> 00:08:57.920
So these cases actually
all did go to juries

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.110
and the expectation was
they all would go to juries

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:03.640
and the jury right wasn't
just a right of a defendant,

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:06.330
this happened in every case
because the public had a right

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:10.180
to do justice itself as
well as seeing justice done.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:11.970
- [Kim] I have so many
questions about this.

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:16.228
So for one thing, do you
think our current system

00:09:16.228 --> 00:09:20.430
is an improvement on this
older system of juries

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:22.984
and not so much plea bargaining?

00:09:22.984 --> 00:09:26.853
Also, why is there is so
much plea bargaining today?

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.587
- [Fisher] At the time of the founding,

00:09:29.587 --> 00:09:33.750
there were no deals to be
made short of a full trial.

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:37.500
That's a huge contrast
with today where over 90%

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:41.910
of criminal cases in this country
are not resolved by trial,

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:43.910
but rather by what's
called a plea bargain,

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:46.310
which is a defendant coming in and saying,

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:49.001
look, I will plead guilty
to a lesser offense

00:09:49.001 --> 00:09:51.390
or to the same offense you're charging

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:54.000
but with a guarantee of lesser punishment,

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.040
as long as you don't require
me to go through a full trial.

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:00.280
And so the idea is that because the system

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:03.338
has grown so large and
there are so many cases,

00:10:03.338 --> 00:10:07.300
that it's a relief to the
prosecution and the court system

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:09.490
not to have to have a trial in every case.

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:11.282
And the benefit to the defendant is

00:10:11.282 --> 00:10:13.430
that he gets a slightly better deal

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:16.670
or is able to plea to a lesser crime.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:18.700
- [Bibas] Over the course
of the 19th century,

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.520
prosecutors are really taking
over most of these cases.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:24.800
It goes from a few percent
to the lion's share of cases.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.102
Then defense lawyers
get hired more and more

00:10:27.102 --> 00:10:28.600
to counteract that.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:31.200
Once you have prosecutors and
defense lawyers on both sides,

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.100
and the Sixth Amendment
guarantees you the right

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.570
to hire a defense lawyer,
that's how it's understood

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:37.501
in the 18th and 19th centuries.

00:10:37.501 --> 00:10:39.150
The prosecutors and defense lawyers

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:40.500
develop rules of evidence,

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:42.370
and they develop procedures,

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:43.860
and jury instructions,

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:45.590
and they argue motions.

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:47.507
And so they get more active

00:10:47.507 --> 00:10:50.720
and the judge gets more
passive or reactive.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.040
So the judge isn't pushing
the case along as much

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:56.080
and the prosecutor and defense
lawyer are slowing it down.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.740
Now that seems like it makes it fairer,

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:59.670
but once it gets to a certain point

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:03.330
of being slow the prosecutors
want to get rid of the cases.

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:05.120
They have too many of them.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:06.700
The judges, they have a bunch

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:08.980
of civil cases they need to get rid of.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.640
So the prosecutors and the
judges and the defense lawyers

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:14.840
all get together and find
ways to bargain the case away

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:19.060
rather than spending now its
days on each case for trial.

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:21.370
So from their point of
view it makes sense.

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:24.630
They have a faster system
and everyone goes home happy

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:26.157
and the prosecutor can pursue more cases

00:11:26.157 --> 00:11:28.670
and the defendant gets a lighter deal,

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:29.990
but the prosecution makes sure there's

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:31.940
some conviction and sentence.

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:33.520
But for the point of view of the public,

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:34.777
the public doesn't see justice done

00:11:34.777 --> 00:11:37.490
and the public doesn't
trust these plea bargains.

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:38.520
But at the end of the day,

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:40.060
what the defense lawyer's mostly doing

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:42.740
is not making sure there's
a speedy and public trial,

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:47.600
but bargaining 95% of cases
away before there's a trial.

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:50.250
- [Kim] What happens if the defendant

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:53.373
can't afford a legal counsel?

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:56.698
- [Fisher] The history of the
right to counsel is actually

00:11:56.698 --> 00:11:57.780
one of the more interesting components

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:00.670
of the Sixth Amendment because many

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.410
of the components have deep
roots in the history of England,

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:07.658
and so were rights that Englishmen enjoyed

00:12:07.658 --> 00:12:10.740
for many many years before
they were brought over

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:12.200
to the colonies and became part

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:14.540
of our Constitution of the United States.

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:16.430
The right to counsel is
a little bit different

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:17.773
in that respect.

00:12:17.773 --> 00:12:21.608
Criminal defendants in
England and other countries

00:12:21.608 --> 00:12:23.660
around the time of our founding

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:25.810
often did not have a right to counsel

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:28.380
of any kind, whether they
could pay for it or not.

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.550
And so this was a bit of an innovation

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:32.760
on the part of the Framers,

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:36.567
recognizing that all the other
rights they were identifying

00:12:36.567 --> 00:12:40.130
to defend yourself in court
might not amount to much

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:41.780
if you didn't have expert assistance

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:44.380
or at least the right to
obtain expert assistance.

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:46.740
- [Kim] So you described a lot of changes

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:50.450
to how our legal system
works over the years.

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:53.440
What do you think would have
surprised the Framers most

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.120
about how our legal system works today?

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:58.750
- [Bibas] I think they
would have been shocked

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:02.129
that a system that was meant to slow down

00:13:02.129 --> 00:13:06.590
and check governmental power
and protect overreaching

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:10.744
and protect the people's
role has been so subverted

00:13:10.744 --> 00:13:12.710
that the people are involved

00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:15.590
in a few percent of cases.

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:18.550
They designed a system that's
slower and less efficient

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:21.320
by design than the criminal justice system

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:23.450
of continental Europe
because they were worried

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:27.850
about the king and the Crown
pushing around citizens

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:29.260
and abusing their power.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:32.650
They wanted grand juries to
authorize every criminal charge.

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:36.170
Well, we've largely gotten
around that in most cases.

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:38.440
They wanted petty juries to ensure

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:40.630
that every prosecutor's
charge was justified

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:42.680
and that the legislature's criminal laws

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:44.130
weren't being applied too broadly.

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:47.830
And again, prosecutors
now have so many threats

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:50.440
and tools and sentences that
they can make almost everybody

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:53.440
give up their jury-trial rights.

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:55.050
But the Framers also weren't dealing

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:57.100
with the volume of cases
we're talking about,

00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:00.080
and they weren't focused
primarily on the violent crimes.

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:01.700
The federal crimes they were dealing with

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:05.710
were seditious libel,
criticizing the government,

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:07.770
violations of the revenue laws.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:10.100
So, it's also fair to point out

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:12.810
that they did not expect that these rights

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:14.630
were mainly gonna be limitations

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:18.600
that benefited people in
rape and murder cases.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:20.650
A lot of these protections
are mainly designed

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:22.800
to make sure that the innocent people

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.580
get vindicated against unjust charges.

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:27.970
And they might be a little bit surprised

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:30.350
that almost all the
litigation is by people

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:33.100
who may be factually guilty.

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:34.780
- [Fisher] The next thing
that would've surprised

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:36.900
the Framers a great deal, I think,

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:39.431
is the role of the jury today.

00:14:39.431 --> 00:14:43.318
The jury-trial right mentioned
in the Sixth Amendment

00:14:43.318 --> 00:14:46.870
is actually one of the
only things mentioned twice

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:48.573
in the Constitution.

00:14:48.573 --> 00:14:50.270
The right to a jury trial
is actually mentioned

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:52.770
in the body of the Constitution itself,

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:55.220
in article four and then
it's mentioned again

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:56.150
in the Sixth Amendment.

00:14:56.150 --> 00:14:57.990
And the reason why is because the jury

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:01.400
were truly the people's
check on the court system,

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:05.930
and in particular, on
the prosecutorial power.

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:10.930
So serving on a jury, to
the Framers, was an honor.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:16.440
It was an act of deep political
participation and meaning.

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:19.830
In a way that we joke
about jury service today

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:21.640
as being something of a nuisance,

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:24.110
they viewed it as almost as important

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:26.440
as voting for the
President on Election Day.

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:30.080
- [Kim] By guaranteeing
speedy public trials by jury

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:31.830
and due process of law,

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:34.610
the Sixth Amendment ensures that citizens,

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:38.180
not the government,
control criminal justice.

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:41.710
But today, as Professor
Fisher and Judge Bibas note,

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:44.070
the Framers might be surprised to learn

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:46.780
that due to the rise of plea bargaining,

00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:50.970
only a small percentage of
cases go to trial by jury,

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:53.694
circumventing this process.

00:15:53.694 --> 00:15:55.310
To learn more about the
Sixth Amendment, visit the

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:58.680
National Constitution Center's
Interactive Constitution

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:00.300
and Khan Academy's resources

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:01.953
on U.S. government and politics.

